WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.200
Gary W. Goldstein, Producer/Author/Instructor:
At that time Michael Keaton was the lead actor.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:10.120
Well the deal went awry. I don't know the
details but the deal went awry so production

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.170
was sidelined…

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:16.360
Film Courage: What was the original version
of Pretty Woman?

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:21.250
Gary: The original version was titled ‘Three
Thousand’ spelled out which was the number

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:24.680
of dollars that Edward [Richard Gere] pays
Vivian [Julia Roberts] for her companionship

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:27.460
for the week.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:36.839
It was a drama, it was not a picture where
boy and girl end up together and in some ways

00:00:36.839 --> 00:00:39.039
it was a much more realistic story.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:48.339
And I think it was very reflective of the
writers…sort of the world that he lived

00:00:48.339 --> 00:00:54.530
in and the way he was feeling about things
at the time.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:01:01.690
But there was something undeniably here's
what I would say about it Jonathan [J. F.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:06.680
Lawton, screenwriter] when he was in film
school he kept writing these very complex

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:12.579
really interesting leading women characters
and I remember one of our early conversations

00:01:12.579 --> 00:01:16.690
before we started working together he let
on that one of his professors presumably a

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:21.880
male as I recall wagged his finger at him
and basically said you know leave that alone

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:27.659
let the women write women characters you focus
on blah blah which was like waving a red flag

00:01:27.659 --> 00:01:33.350
in front of a bull. John one of the things
I’d say that we shared in common was a certain

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:39.600
reverence for women and that showed up in
all of his scripts no matter how flawed and

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.930
they were all flawed but they were absolutely
magnificent and one of the things I would

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:50.659
say about his character his women elite lead
women characters was that they were inevitably

00:01:50.659 --> 00:01:55.929
the moral superior in every given any given
story and I would say that was one of the

00:01:55.929 --> 00:02:03.189
things that really stood out for me in this
sort of grittier world of Hollywood or the

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:06.600
collision of these two worlds the investment
banker from New York and the girl from the

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:14.760
streets of Hollywood coming together this
unlikely duo where he had a certain wound

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:21.320
a certain skepticism a certain you know agenda
based on his relationship with his dad and

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:29.650
his life experience his current girlfriend
and then she despite her circumstances was

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:39.780
this ineffable spirit who had this sort of
moral code she was just a delightful character

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:46.180
who's making the best of her circumstances
and there was something so alluring to me

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:53.640
about her in this original script Three Thousand.
So while it didn't end well I just saw that

00:02:53.640 --> 00:03:05.900
the relationship and who she was was such
a unexpected construction and I figure I’m

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:11.120
like a really good audience I’m like I am
almost like the perfect audience to read a

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:19.080
script I don't read it when I get a script
I never read it as if i'm the producer I try

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.200
not to read it as up on the director or the
financier or the distributor or any of those

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.780
hats the closest thing I would say is I try
to read it as if I’m going to be the lead

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:36.720
actor but more importantly I try to read it
as if I am the audience because that's ultimately

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:48.190
who I am right and that script was was I a
must-see story for me so my belief in it was

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:52.760
a thousand percent it was like it wouldn't
matter how many no's I heard that was going

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:56.970
to be I was going to do anything possible
to get that film made and originally it was

00:03:56.970 --> 00:04:04.360
intended to be made as a scripted as a lower
budgeted you know several million dollar independent

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:11.020
film because it was set in just a room in
a hotel right we didn't have a lot of production

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:24.430
value to worry about but I guess the the authenticity
of both the lead characters was so undeniable.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:30.570
It was written like he knew these people deeply
and then the supporting cast Jonathan had

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:37.980
a gift as well there's no such thing as a
secondary character they may be supporting

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:44.270
they may not have a lot of dialogue or page
count but no one in that story is not important

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:53.460
even the hotel manager even the elevator operator
even before it became a comedy you could see

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:58.520
we were witnessing with and through the eyes
of these people and they were so interesting

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:05.930
in their own right so it was I don't know
if that answers your question but it was absolutely

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:11.780
a mesmerizing read how obsessed were you with
the screenplay when you first read it I wish

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:16.510
I could take it back to the moment I closed
the script cover because it was a script that

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:25.160
I know that if I can't stop reading which
I didn't I read it cover to cover in one sitting

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:31.139
which is not that extraordinary but it's a
good sign but what I know when I closed the

00:05:31.139 --> 00:05:38.270
screen cover I was speechless like I had just
experienced something, probably the most special

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:47.260
script at that young juncture of my career
that I’d read to date and I could see it

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:53.160
it's it's there's something about a brilliant
screenplay that draws you and it takes you

00:05:53.160 --> 00:06:00.880
on a journey that is so detailed and and emotive
where the characters are so different or unexpected

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:09.060
that you feel yourself literally being pulled
into the story and

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:18.630
that was the case with Three Thousand it was
certainly I was devastated at the end but

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:23.170
apart from the being devastated because of
the reality of the story and the experience

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:30.040
of the characters when I closed that script
however I was a bit stunned into silence like

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:35.010
I know I have a movie I for the first time
you know this is like I want this to be a

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:46.770
movie I produce it's the first time I said
that to myself and I don't really you know

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:50.930
honestly it's almost like you go blank after
that and I don't remember all the particular

00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:56.210
conversations that we had but I know I immediately
reached out to Jonathan and said I am so happy

00:06:56.210 --> 00:07:04.419
we're working together this is this is a special
script are fascinating creatures and sometimes

00:07:04.419 --> 00:07:07.790
there's something that really stands out it
might be a particular character it might be

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:13.230
the world it might be the descriptions it
could be dialogue it could there was something

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:22.540
about Three Thousand where the whole of it
was just right and all of it drew me in.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:27.540
What was interesting was when I finished reading
it I was so excited and yet I was also very

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:34.949
pragmatic I realized I was still relatively
new in the business I mean certainly I hadn't

00:07:34.949 --> 00:07:40.650
produced except for a couple of micro budget
little you know but I hadn't produced anything

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:48.960
the world knew and Jonathan was still relatively
unknown he was not yet an established name

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:54.800
but what I held in my hand merited a lot of
attention and the question was how do I get

00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:02.260
from here to there how do I create that energy
and the one thing that occurred to me was

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:09.520
I think this is such an artful compelling
beautifully crafted story that it lives at

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:17.930
the level of the early like extremely rarefied
atmosphere that was Sundance institute so

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:24.570
I called up I had befriended Michelle Satter
who sat at the right hand of Robert Redford

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:29.910
since day one building the institute building
the festival and all that it had become and

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:36.309
they had all these beautiful labs and workshops
and they had one called the production lab

00:08:36.309 --> 00:08:42.860
where they would pick I don't know a limited
number eight ten perhaps projects and invite

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:49.800
them once a year it was maybe a week-long
event and you would go to Utah and you would

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:56.320
workshop the project meaning you would have
a level talent show up all these resource

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:03.029
people would show for free editors cinematographers
actors you name it and so you would take that

00:09:03.029 --> 00:09:09.519
script and break it down and you'd start videotaping
scenes with these actors and sort of trying

00:09:09.519 --> 00:09:21.329
to you know find what's working how to improve
it etc so I sent it to Michelle and she quite

00:09:21.329 --> 00:09:26.050
quickly called me back and said I want this
project I want you I want to invite you guys

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:37.890
to come as the writer producer so we did and
it was one of those exalted moments that you'll

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:42.999
never forget because when you're still relatively
you know you're you're at the lower end of

00:09:42.999 --> 00:09:48.180
the ladder you're climbing the ladder and
then you're invited to Sundance already amazing

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:53.959
but beyond that one day I remember I could
I see the scene I’m sitting there and there

00:09:53.959 --> 00:09:58.930
are actors and there are the video cameras
and Jonathan's there and a bunch of other

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:05.509
people and we have these wonderful actors
playing Edward and Vivian and we shot a scene

00:10:05.509 --> 00:10:15.399
and suddenly I hear a voice whispering notes
in my ear what if and I look just like I thought

00:10:15.399 --> 00:10:22.100
I was dreaming this man that was whispering
in my ear was Paul Hirsch editor of Star Wars

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:29.589
editor of you know an Oscar winning you know
like 50 of the greatest films ever and he

00:10:29.589 --> 00:10:38.399
is offering me creative notes it was like
I died and gone to heaven, it was a magical

00:10:38.399 --> 00:10:45.930
experience to see this sort of come to life
in that venue and of course Hollywood took

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:50.699
notice that was the whole idea well one of
the inciting ideas was I want people to I

00:10:50.699 --> 00:10:57.449
want this in the trades I want people to see
that by association this is a quality project

00:10:57.449 --> 00:11:05.009
and that and it worked the phone started ringing
people were very interested it started to

00:11:05.009 --> 00:11:14.689
have this sort of create this this cause celeb
this buzz this energy 

00:11:14.689 --> 00:11:19.829
the script had a consistent it's been taught
in film schools it had that sort of consistent

00:11:19.829 --> 00:11:26.279
response it was one of those rare scripts
that sort of hits everybody where they live

00:11:26.279 --> 00:11:33.230
everyone can relate to the humanity of this
story and so we were very blessed that way

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:37.709
and that you know obviously like every film
it travels a very twisted journey and it goes

00:11:37.709 --> 00:11:42.389
off the cliff here and it dies by a thousand
paper cuts over there and and so on but you

00:11:42.389 --> 00:11:51.819
stay with it and I don't know if i've ever
shared the I sent it wide option to best run

00:11:51.819 --> 00:11:55.879
and then I got it back out and then I optioned
it to new regency and we were still stalled

00:11:55.879 --> 00:12:02.980
we couldn't get it financed or cast Richard
had Richard Garry turned the project down

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:08.600
and during that time back when I was at restaurant
I had friends Mark and Scott who produced

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:14.589
Mystic Pizza which is just such a brilliant
film and when the lights came up of this little

00:12:14.589 --> 00:12:20.430
friends and family screening to get feedback
before they locked picture I had I had no

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:25.959
criticism to offer but I saw this girl on
the screen and I said I don't know who she

00:12:25.959 --> 00:12:31.739
is but will you introduce me and that was
Julia Roberts and they did they were very

00:12:31.739 --> 00:12:39.199
kind and Julia and her then manager read it
and was attached for three years before we

00:12:39.199 --> 00:12:40.639
actually went into production

00:12:40.639 --> 00:12:50.139
but unexpectedly because Hollywood is nothing
if not non-linear right it's full of surprise

00:12:50.139 --> 00:12:54.989
so I sent it to someone I had known when he
was a junior a lesser executive somewhere

00:12:54.989 --> 00:13:01.920
and then he became a senior vp at touchstone
and I sent it as a writing sample saying this

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:08.309
is not Disney material it's about a prostitute
but you're going to absolutely flip you're

00:13:08.309 --> 00:13:11.679
going to fall in love with the craft this
writer is someone you need to know and we're

00:13:11.679 --> 00:13:15.189
going to come in a week heads you'll read
it we'll come in next week and we will pitch

00:13:15.189 --> 00:13:22.290
you a couple of more Disney appropriate stories
it was only several days before the phone

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:30.929
rang and and and the call was we want to buy
it and I was taken a little back and I said

00:13:30.929 --> 00:13:39.309
gosh are we talking about the same script
well we were and I mean there's a there's

00:13:39.309 --> 00:13:43.910
a lesson in this for everyone in this business
like you cannot know what's happening behind

00:13:43.910 --> 00:13:50.670
the curtain you don't know all the forces
or influences that are at play Disney touchstone

00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:58.660
in this case had a the wonderful project what
about bob and it was going in depression with

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:02.910
Garry Marshall directing and Dianne Critten
and casting in the full Laura Siskind line

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:10.619
producing and they had the full team and at
that time Michael Keaton was the lead actor

00:14:10.619 --> 00:14:19.249
well the deal went awry I don't know the details
but the deal went awry so production was sidelined

00:14:19.249 --> 00:14:26.079
and they thought gosh we have Garry we have
this team assembled and they looked at our

00:14:26.079 --> 00:14:32.970
script and thought great we'll create a marriage.
so there was this meeting a week later that

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:38.899
was no longer a pitch meeting it was about
we want to buy this script and we just want

00:14:38.899 --> 00:14:45.320
to know can you lighten it the question actually
from the president of the studio was on the

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:49.999
Disney lightning scale this is a foreign we'd
like it to be a seven can you do that and

00:14:49.999 --> 00:14:59.449
I answered absolutely yes we can do that because
it was pretty evident what they were asking

00:14:59.449 --> 00:15:03.019
and

00:15:03.019 --> 00:15:09.709
as a result we not only inherited I mean I’m
not so brilliant that I would have thought

00:15:09.709 --> 00:15:16.850
of Garry Marshall directing this particular
film and we were fortunate because i'd never

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:23.389
had a big studio a major director and a deep
pocket so I went back to Richard Gere's agent

00:15:23.389 --> 00:15:30.579
Ed Lamotto and said please because Ed loved
the project would you go back to Richard now

00:15:30.579 --> 00:15:34.889
and the studio backed it and they made him
the sort of the godfather offer that you couldn't

00:15:34.889 --> 00:15:42.579
refuse and so Richard finally said yes and
the beauty of that was they've been auditioning

00:15:42.579 --> 00:15:51.639
and meeting and screen testing many many many
actors both for the male and female lead and

00:15:51.639 --> 00:15:56.259
you know it's it's there's sort of a lot of
it's it's public information there's you know

00:15:56.259 --> 00:16:03.139
the long list of name actors and actresses
who who who sort of paraded through and fortunately

00:16:03.139 --> 00:16:10.160
many of them declined either passed on it
or whatever for whatever reason it didn't

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:18.540
happen but Julia was not on their radar she
was like an unknown actress at the time she

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:25.819
had shot steel magnolias but it hadn't come
out and she was so you know Mystic Pizza wonderful

00:16:25.819 --> 00:16:34.129
movie but she was not yet sort of well known
to either the public or or the studios but

00:16:34.129 --> 00:16:42.199
now with Richard if you have your anchor tenant
right then it was like okay now this is possible

00:16:42.199 --> 00:16:46.730
just maybe I just wanted them to screen test
together that would have been all my dream

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:58.220
right and so I you know I lobbied and said
you know this is but it had to be Gary Marshall

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:04.850
and the idea was Garry meet her alone just
the two of you and you're going to fall in

00:17:04.850 --> 00:17:13.150
love and that happened so when Garry Marshall
versus Gary W. Goldstein says to the c-suite

00:17:13.150 --> 00:17:18.459
of Hollywood of a major studio this is my
girl I want to screen test it happened and

00:17:18.459 --> 00:17:26.769
once they did it was it was so undeniable
so the journey of a film is so unknowable

00:17:26.769 --> 00:17:31.919
so unpredictable and that we ended up with
our dreamcast and it turns out a dream director

00:17:31.919 --> 00:17:40.090
I didn't know was even in my dream is it's
you know it's the stuff that you can't you

00:17:40.090 --> 00:17:41.750
can't make up just happens.

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:41.970
